--- body ---
## Context

Reported from a climate-finance-het session on 2026-11-12 (DOIfetch v0.3.0,
batch mode, `--source all`, Zotero check on by default).

Observation: work 10.1016/j.worlddev.2018.12.011 (Peterson and Skovgaard 2019)
has a Zotero item with no attachment. The batch run counted it in
`Success: 5`, wrote no file, and removed it from the retry list. A single run
with `--no-check-zotero` downloaded the publisher PDF at once.

## Diagnosis (from the code at origin/main)

1. `zotero.zotero_lookup` (`_match_by_doi`, `_match_by_title`) matches any
   non-deleted item with the DOI or a near-exact title; it never asks whether
   the item has a file attachment. A metadata-only item counts as held.
2. In batch mode, `cli.py` (around line 400) appends a `skipped` result to
   `successful_records`: it is counted in the progress line's `Success:` and
   in the success rate, and `update_source_files` drops it from the
   `.txt` retry list. The log line says `[SKIPPED]`, the console summary does
   not, so the user sees a success with no file.

Effect: a work the user wants (often exactly the one whose item exists but
whose PDF is missing) silently leaves the retry list without a download.

## Proposed fix

- Count a Zotero match as held only when the item has a PDF attachment
  (`itemAttachments` with `contentType = 'application/pdf'`, not deleted);
  a metadata-only match proceeds to the fetch, logged as such.
- Report skips separately from successes in the batch summary
  (`Success / Skipped / Failed`), keeping held works off the retry list
  only when they really are held.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] A test: an item with the DOI and no attachment does not skip; an item
      with a PDF attachment does.
- [ ] Batch summary prints skipped works apart from successes.
- [ ] Retry list keeps a work whose Zotero item has no PDF.
